2. WHAT YOU MAY DO

   a. Build an unlimited number of applications with it, whether for yourself
      or for a client, and whether internal or public.

   b. Charge your users for access to those applications, including as a paid
      product or a subscription.

   c. Modify it however you like. You do not have to publish your changes, and
      you do not have to credit this template.

   d. Hand the modified source to a client as part of delivering work to them,
      provided the result is an application and not the template itself. If
      that client will go on to build further applications from it, they need
      their own license.

   e. Keep using every version you received, forever, even if your access to
      updates ends.


3. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

   a. Resell, relicense, sublicense or give away the template, or any part of
      it, as a template, theme, starter, boilerplate, UI kit or component
      library — whether modified or not, and whether paid or free.

   b. Publish it in a public repository, gist, tutorial download, course
      material or asset marketplace in a form from which it could be reused as
      a template.

   c. Build a product whose main value is the template itself — a page builder,
      a theme marketplace, or a service that generates applications from it for
      third parties.

   d. Remove or alter this license file from copies of the source.

   The line is the difference between shipping something built with this and
   distributing the thing you built it with.


